{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"I'm tracing through the overflow propagation logic for the html{overflow-x:hidden} case—checking how clipBox resolves when html isn't the documentElement, and how scrollerOf walks up from body's parent since body's overflowX is visible, leading to a null scroller and an offscreenLeft check.\n\nThen I'm considering mixed cases: when html{overflow:hidden} and body{overflow-x:hidden} together, body becomes the actual clip box since rootStyle isn't propagating. I'm verifying margin-left:-400px content gets correctly flagged as clipped by body's rect, accounting for body's default 8px margin, and now I'm working through how documentWidth is computed from the max of root and body scrollWidth when html has overflow-x hidden.\n\nWhen both html and body set overflow-x:hidden, body isn't propagating so it becomes the clip box itself, and content pushed beyond it still gets caught as overflow. I'm also checking the offscreenLeft logic — it excludes body itself but should still catch body's children with negative transforms, and I'm wondering about content inside non-body wrappers with their own overflow settings.\n\nIf a wrapper is pushed off-screen via negative margin with overflow hidden, offscreenLeft on the content inside gets skipped since it has a scroller, but the wrapper itself should get flagged as offscreen since its own rect has no containing scroller above it. I'm now checking whether the same logic holds for content inside an auto-scrolling container that's itself shifted off-screen.\n\nFor screen-reader-only detection, I'm now considering honest overflow:hidden cases like a collapsed accordion panel with height:0, which should be caught similarly to tiny 1x1 clipped boxes or clip-path inset tricks.\n\nI'm also considering the older rect(1px,1px,1px,1px) sr-only pattern alongside rect(0,0,0,0), and noting an edge case: `clip` only truly applies to absolutely positioned elements, so a static element with that clip value might get misclassified as sr-only when it isn't actually clipped, since browsers may still report the computed clip value regardless of positioning.\n\nOther clip-path shapes like inset(100%) or zero-area polygons also effectively hide elements, and sizing edge cases like a zero-width overflow-hidden wrapper still count as genuinely invisible content, so these don't create false positives. Now I'm checking the display:contents case with nowrap text to see how it behaves.\n\nFor display:contents elements, getBoundingClientRect returns 0x0 but computed overflow still follows whatever the author specified, so unless overflow:hidden was explicitly set, the check still works since the text node rects report actual size. I need to trace how the ancestor walk handles a position:absolute ancestor in the sr-only detection logic.\n\nChecking opacity:0 cases too — those are genuinely hidden but not caught by width/height logic, which matters for fade-in animations where content stays invisible until scrolled into view. I also want to verify that inline elements with their own box dimensions inside collapsed parents still get measured correctly, and that zero-sized images are properly excluded from the \"shown\" set, before confirming the ancestor-inclusion logic only applies to elements that are both present in content and currently shown.\n\nI'm checking parentOf for slotted/shadow DOM cases, which seem fine, then working through the N15 stepped-scroll test: with a narrow table and a single link near the left edge, max right stays far below extent.right, so stepped correctly evaluates false. I'm verifying that the last target must actually reach the right edge for stepped to be true.\n\nIf the content extending past the last link is plain text, the extent could exceed the link's right edge, causing a false negative for stepped even when scrolling behaves reasonably — which I think is acceptable since it fails closed. I'm also confirming that revealing each target via scrollIntoView should progressively shift the right edge within the allowed span if the sequence is genuinely incremental.\n\nOne issue I need to flag: targets get sorted by left position for stepping, but actual tab order follows DOM order, not visual order, so there's a potential mismatch there worth checking.\n\nTracing through non-monotonic right-edge cases, I'm checking whether the span-coverage check could produce a false pass when a wide target sits between narrower ones — sorting by left doesn't guarantee rights increase, so the diff check might miss gaps while still appearing conservative in most scenarios I'm testing.\n\nLooking at closedShadowRoots handling: the catch returning null marks document-level scope as unscanned appropriately, and the traversal through shadow roots, children, content documents, and template content correctly excludes user-agent shadow roots via their shadowRootType. Declarative shadow DOM inside templates being treated as unscanned is overly strict but acceptable since that content isn't actually rendered.\n\nI'm now checking whether this closed-root counting also applies to the design-probe copy/spacing check, and whether a width-screen failure there counts as a handoff failure during the wireframe phase, which means I need to look at the pre-handoff-check logic and whether closed-root counting applies in the JS-off scenario.\n\n","type":"thinking"}],"id":"msg_7218d4b8907d4bf4bb2a218e89b667b8","model":"claude-opus-5-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":11770,"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01DPkzkRuJaRjVw8TvQQyMje","agent_id":"ab187913895d29e5f","session_id":"f0bd072d-d495-4454-a967-3e00aaf8b556","uuid":"e4581120-9913-4b9d-b4a8-e0ae200720ff","timestamp":"2026-10-18T02:58:35.712Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Part A gate diff review"}
