{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_017kzBb4qwK822Cd5T13eFmq","type":"tool_result","content":"26\t# R11 (Asad panel C4): secret-bearing and junk names are never packaged or hashed (environment files, OS junk, private\n27\t# keys and certificates, credential dotfiles, editor swap and backup files).\n28\tJUNK_OR_SECRET = re.compile(r'(?:\\.env(?:\\..*)?|\\.DS_Store|Thumbs\\.db|desktop\\.ini|\\.npmrc|\\.netrc|\\.pypirc|id_(?:rsa|dsa|ecdsa|ed25519)(?:\\.pub)?|.*\\.(?:pem|key|p12|pfx|swp|swo)|.*~)')\n29\t\n30\t\n31\t# R11 panel D1 (decision D): packaging is an explicit allowlist. A packaged or hashed file must have one of these\n32\t# suffixes (the ZIP itself only at ARCHIVE) and no path part may be VCS or editor debris; JUNK_OR_SECRET is still\n33\t# refused on top, so `.env.md` or `id_rsa.txt` fail too. Adding a new file type is a deliberate edit here.\n34\tALLOWED_SUFFIXES = {'.md', '.mjs', '.json', '.sh', '.sha256', '.txt', '.py', '.diff', '.internal', '.log'}\n35\tDENIED_PARTS = {'.git', '.gitignore', '.gitattributes', '.gitmodules', '.hg', '.svn', '.idea', '.vscode', '.history', '.ssh', '.gnupg', '.aws'}  # R12 M12: credential folders\n36\tJUNK_OR_SECRET_STEM = re.compile(r'(?:\\.env(?:\\..*)?|id_(?:rsa|dsa|ecdsa|ed25519)(?:\\.pub)?)\\.[A-Za-z0-9]+')\n37\t# R12 M12: allowlisted suffixes do not launder secret names. Any path part that is an environment file (.env, .envrc,\n38\t# .env-prod ...), an editor lock (.#name), a credential file name with any suffixes (credentials.json, secrets.yaml.md,\n39\t# service-account.json, token.json, .npmrc.json, .git-credentials.txt) or a key or certificate followed by more\n40\t# suffixes (private.key.json) is refused. R13 N10 replaces the R12 plural allowance: see CREDENTIAL_WORD below.\n41\tSECRET_ANY_SUFFIX = re.compile(r'(?:\\.env[^/]*|\\.#.*|(?:credentials?|secrets?|service[-_]account(?:[-_][^.]*)?|token|\\.npmrc|\\.netrc|\\.pypirc|\\.git-credentials)(?:\\..*)?|.*\\.(?:pem|key|p12|pfx)(?:\\..*)?)', re.I)\n42\t\n43\t\n44\t# R13 N10 / panel q2: a credential word anywhere in a path part (prefix, infix or suffix; singular or plural) refuses\n45\t# it, except three reviewed tool, test and evidence names and a design-token `tokens.json` that is (a) declared by the\n46\t# `handoff.json` beside it (`tokens.json.path` ends with this path and its sha256 matches) and (b) valid design-token\n47\t# content: every leaf is a node with `$value`, and no credential keys (access_token, refresh_token, client_secret ...).\n48\tCREDENTIAL_WORD = re.compile(r'credential|secret|service[-_.]?account|token|password|passwd|api[-_.]?key|private[-_.]?key|access[-_.]?key', re.I)\n49\tCREDENTIAL_NAME_REVIEWED = {'token-references.mjs', 'token-validation.test.mjs', 'secret-scan.json'}\n50\tCREDENTIAL_KEYS = {'access_token', 'refresh_token', 'client_secret', 'id_token', 'password', 'api_key', 'apikey', 'private_key', 'secret'}\n51\t\n52\t\n53\tdef design_tokens_ok(path, rel):\n54\t    try:\n55\t        data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n56\t        handoff = json.loads((path.parent / 'handoff.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n57\t    except (OSError, ValueError):\n58\t        return False\n59\t    declared = ((handoff.get('tokens') or {}).get('json') or {}) if isinstance(handoff, dict) else {}\n60\t    where = str(declared.get('path', '')).replace('\\\\', '/')\n61\t    if not (where == rel or where.endswith('/' + rel)) or declared.get('sha256') != hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest():\n62\t        return False\n63\t    values = []\n64\t\n65\t    def walk(node):\n66\t        if not isinstance(node, dict):\n67\t            return False\n68\t        if any(str(k).lower() in CREDENTIAL_KEYS for k in node):\n69\t            return False\n70\t        if '$value' in node:\n71\t            values.append(1)\n72\t            return not isinstance(node['$value'], dict) or walk_value(node['$value'])\n73\t        return all(walk(v) for k, v in node.items() if not str(k).startswith('$'))\n74\t\n75\t    def walk_value(value):\n76\t        return not any(str(k).lower() in CREDENTIAL_KEYS for k in value) and all(walk_value(v) for v in value.values() if isinstance(v, dict))\n77\t    return walk(data) and bool(values)\n78\t\n79\t\n80\tdef credential_name(name, root):\n81\t    parts = Path(name).parts\n82\t    for i, part in enumerate(parts):\n83\t        if not CREDENTIAL_WORD.search(part):\n84\t            continue\n85\t        last = i == len(parts) - 1\n86\t        if last and part in CREDENTIAL_NAME_REVIEWED:\n87\t            continue\n88\t        if last and part == 'tokens.json' and design_tokens_ok(Path(root) / name, Path(name).as_posix()):\n89\t            continue\n90\t        return True\n91\t    return False\n92\t\n93\t\n94\t# R13 panel q2: a stdlib content screen for obvious secrets in every packaged or hashed text file (names alone miss a\n95\t# key pasted into an ordinary file). Patterns are assembled here so this file does not match itself.\n96\tSECRET_CONTENT = re.compile('|'.join([\n97\t    '-----BEGIN [A-Z ]*' + 'PRIVATE KEY-----', 'AK' + 'IA[0-9A-Z]{16}', 'gh' + 'p_[A-Za-z0-9]{36}', 'github' + '_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{22,}',\n98\t    'xo' + 'x[baprs]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{10,}', 's' + 'k-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{32,}', 'AI' + 'za[0-9A-Za-z_-]{35}',\n99\t    '\"(?:refresh' + '_token|client' + '_secret|access' + '_token)\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\"[^\"]{8,}\"']))\n100\t\n101\t\n102\tdef secret_content(path):\n103\t    try:\n104\t        return bool(SECRET_CONTENT.search(path.read_bytes().decode('utf-8', 'ignore')))\n105\t    except OSError:\n106\t        return False\n107\t\n108\t\n109\tdef junk_or_secret(name, root=None):\n110\t    parts = Path(name).parts\n111\t    if any(JUNK_OR_SECRET.fullmatch(part) or part in DENIED_PARTS for part in parts):\n112\t        return True\n113\t    if parts and JUNK_OR_SECRET_STEM.fullmatch(parts[-1]):\n114\t        return True\n115\t    if any(SECRET_ANY_SUFFIX.fullmatch(part) for part in parts):\n116\t        return True\n117\t    if credential_name(name, ROOT if root is None else root):\n118\t        return True\n119\t    return Path(name).suffix not in ALLOWED_SUFFIXES and name != ARCHIVE\n120\t\n121\t\n122\tdef files(root):\n123\t    paths = sorted(root.rglob('*'))\n124\t    if any(p.is_symlink() for p in paths):\n125\t        raise ValueError('symlinks are forbidden in package input')\n126\t    bad = [p for p in paths if NON_PACKAGE & set(p.relative_to(root).parts) or p.suffix == '.pyc']\n127\t    if bad:\n128\t        raise ValueError('cache, dependency or git entry in package input: ' + bad[0].relative_to(root).as_posix())\n129\t    secret = [p for p in paths if (p.is_file() or set(p.relative_to(root).parts) & DENIED_PARTS) and junk_or_secret(p.relative_to(root).as_posix(), root)]\n130\t    if secret:\n131\t        raise ValueError('secret-bearing, junk or non-allowlisted file in package input: ' + secret[0].relative_to(root).as_posix())\n132\t    leaked = [p for p in paths if p.is_file() and secret_content(p)]  # R13 q2: content screen\n133\t    if leaked:\n134\t        raise ValueError('secret-looking content in package input: ' + leaked[0].relative_to(root).as_posix())\n135\t    return [p for p in paths if p.is_file()]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01DPkzkRuJaRjVw8TvQQyMje","agent_id":"ab187913895d29e5f","session_id":"f0bd072d-d495-4454-a967-3e00aaf8b556","uuid":"934c2621-863b-4b04-9c52-6d1f0dc03478","timestamp":"2026-10-18T02:59:57.230Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_017kzBb4qwK822Cd5T13eFmq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Part A gate diff review"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"I'm checking paths against the credential/secret rules - \"credentials.json\", \"my-secrets/\", \"token.txt\", and \".env\" all get refused as expected. Now I'm wondering about edge cases like design token files (\"tokens.css\", \"design-tokens.md\") or legitimate release files containing \"token\" in their name, since the word 'token' triggers the credential match broadly.\n\nI'm considering whether the content screen could miss generic secrets like a `password:` or `api_key:` field inside a sloppily-named config file that doesn't trip the filename check, since the review relies on basename matching plus a content screen that isn't exhaustive.\n\nThen I trace the overflow propagation case where both html and body set overflow-x hidden: html's hidden propagates to the viewport scroller while body becomes its own real clip box, so content pushed outside body gets clipped there and offscreen detection via auto/scroll scrollers gets skipped since body is a hidden overflow, not an auto/scroll one.\n\nI'm also checking that computed styles don't change due to propagation—html stays 'visible' even if body overrides—and examining the edge case where html sets overflow-y:scroll (forcing a scrollbar), which causes overflow-x to compute to 'auto' rather than 'visible', meaning body's own overflow-x setting no longer propagates.\n\nI'm confirming that horizontal overflow detection using scrollWidth comparisons still works correctly even when html has overflow:hidden, since scrollWidth still reflects actual content size regardless of clipping.\n\nFor N02, I'm checking screen-reader-only detection: tiny boxes combined with clipping, zero-rect clip values, or clip-path inset(50%). I'm verifying the regex matches Chrome's computed clip format like \"rect(0px, 0px, 0px, 0px)\", and noting that computed clip values apply regardless of positioning, so even a static element with that declaration would still report it.\n\nOverflow-hidden boxes sized to 1px with overflowing text are the classic sr-only pattern and should be caught correctly, while larger clipped boxes with long text are properly treated as visible. I'm also noting that clip-path: inset(100%) is a common sr-only variant that might get flagged as merely clipped rather than hidden, which is a low-priority false positive rather than a real bypass. Now I'm tracing how the sr-only flag propagates to descendants through ancestor checks.\n\nThat collapsed accordion case is a false positive but not something I need to worry about. Checking visibility handling confirms display:none descendants correctly compute as not shown since their rects collapse to zero, and inSrOnly correctly skips them too since overflow is visible on those ancestors.\n\nNow I'm tracing through how the shown check interacts with slot-aware parent traversal, and verifying that text inside display:contents elements still gets picked up properly through textRects. For the offscreen-left detection in the main loop, I'm confirming that wrapper elements with negative margins correctly flag their content children as offscreen too.\n\nI'm also checking edge cases around zero-height headings, shadow DOM hosts, and fixed/absolute positioned elements, making sure the beyond/within checks properly handle composed tree traversal and that shadow-only hosts still resolve through parentOf correctly.\n\nI'm noting some known gaps: fixed-position descendants inside hidden wrappers, or absolutely-positioned elements whose containing block sits outside the clipping ancestor, could trigger false positives, but I'm deciding to skip fixing those for now. I'm also scrutinizing the N15 stepped-layout logic, particularly how the extent is derived from flattened content rects across target elements.\n\nI'm checking whether reaching the extent's right edge actually guarantees coverage: focusing each target scrolls it minimally into view, and since content between consecutive targets' right edges stays visible as long as the gap doesn't exceed the viewport span, the union of scroll positions should cover the whole area. Tab order itself doesn't matter since users can tab both directions, so what matters is the union of visible regions across all targets — though I need to remember these rects are measured at the current scroll position, which could shift as focus moves.\n\nNow I'm also considering edge cases: Chrome 130+ made scrollers keyboard-focusable by default even without tabindex, which could cause extra focus stops, but that just means the check fails closed rather than missing anything. Checking tabIndex semantics confirms tabindex=\"-1\" correctly evaluates as not focusable. For closed shadow roots, I'm working through whether passing the proxied page object into newCDPSession will satisfy Playwright's instanceof check.\n\nSince Proxy's instanceof relies on the prototype chain via getPrototypeOf, it resolves to true, and the channel property access goes through the get trap correctly, so this should work as the fixture expects. I'm then thinking through the counting failure path, where a null return should map to the document element being unresolved.\n\nThe CDP walk uses DOM.getDocument with pierce and infinite depth, which covers shadow roots and same-process iframe contents, but the failure path itself isn't tested. I'm noting a subtle gap: closed shadow roots are only enumerated once per screen width before resizing, so any closed root created afterward by a resize handler or media query script would be missed — a minor enhancement opportunity rather than a blocker.\n\nUser-agent shadow roots (inputs, selects, video controls) carry shadowRootType 'user-agent' and get properly excluded from the open-shadow traversal, which is correct behavior. The motion detection setup installs a raf counter via init script in the main world for navigation tracking, but its animation snapshot only queries `body *` without piercing open shadow roots — so CSS animations running inside an open shadow tree wouldn't be caught by that selector, though document.getAnimations() itself does span shadow trees.\n\nStill, timer-driven motion inside a shadow root (e.g. a web component using setInterval rather than rAF to animate a carousel) would slip past both detection methods entirely. That's a real gap given the threat model promises open shadow roots are fully covered — worth flagging as a P2 issue.\n\nI should also check whether contentProbe, contrastProbe, and spacing-scale handle composed/shadow traversal consistently, and confirm iframes are excluded by design. Let me look at integrity.mjs and spacing-scale.mjs directly.\n\n","type":"thinking"}],"id":"msg_73e9453f68fe41119864a16403f801ef","model":"claude-opus-5-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":11968,"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01DPkzkRuJaRjVw8TvQQyMje","agent_id":"ab187913895d29e5f","session_id":"f0bd072d-d495-4454-a967-3e00aaf8b556","uuid":"eb8f9597-5a5b-4297-9ee7-c65f1aabe05d","timestamp":"2026-10-18T03:00:56.420Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Part A gate diff review"}
